"ral"
"The tavern keep considers where best to begin.  \"@GWell, first I suppose its
best to explain that the ral'airdn is a feared beast of the woods for it is
one of the greatest pedators of the Vaasa.  Fortunately, it the ral'airdn are
rarely seen any more as they keep to secluded portions of the wood not
travelled often by elf or ent.@n\"
"
E
"ral2"
"@GA part of what makes it so feared is its uncanny ability to blend in with
its surroundings.  Its natural camoflauge is augmented by innate magic that
further hides and protects it.  When it chooses to attack, it can appear to
come out of nowhere in spite of its huge size, larger even then the largest
ents.
"
E
"ral3"
"@GNow, this happened quite a few hundred years back but before the village was
moved up into the trees, we all lived in a peaceful settlement down on the
ground.  It was on a beautiful, clear day, or so I've heard it told, that the
ral'airdn burst into one of the clearings and started laying waste to the
village.  Many were wounded and some even killed before anyone even knew what
was happening.
"
E
"ral4"
"@GNow, you have to understand that, for all its fearsomeness, the ral'airdn
shuns such populated places and the attack was wholly unexpected.  Some knew
that a ral'airdn made its lair a ways off but none ever expected it to attach
the village.  It was Ulandriel who was the first to spring into action.
"
E
"ral5"
"@GUlandriel held the fearsom beast at bay for crucial minutes buying time for
others to react and either take up arms or seek cover.  If not for Ulandriel
there would have been many more deaths.  Ulandriel is said to have leapt into
the fray with the fluid grace of a dolphin swimming asea, deftly sidestepping
the ral'airdn's every attack yet managing to hold its attention with
lightning-quick blows that stung but did not kill the rare creature of the
forest.
"
E
"ral6"
"@GIt was solely through Ulandriel's actions that Tiercen, not yet high druid of
the Vaasa, was able to take the time and cast the powerful spell that was
required to allay the beasts anger.  As it turned out, once the ral'airdn was
calmed, it was only enraged from the inflamation of an envenomed thorn that
had pierced its tough hide and it was mad with brain-fever.  Working quickly,
Ulandriel scoured the woods for the herbs the druid Tiercen required to cure
the wounded beast.  The ral'airdn was moved to a quiet clearing where it was
tended until it was healed then released to return to its home.
"
E
"ral7"
"@GWhile a creature of the wilds could never be tamed, it remembered well the two
elves who saved it and even appeared to develop a fondness, particularly for
Ulandriel and it would occasionally be seen accompanying him on his later
travels.  When, at last, the great beast finally succumbed to advanced age and
lay down for its final rest, it made its final rest in the company of
Ulandriel.  Ulandriel took from it only the horn, returning the rest to the
forest from which it came.  It is that same horn that is set atop the crown of
the world.
"
E
"ral8"
"Finishing her tale, the tavern keep says, \"@GLegend speaks that, when one who
is chosen blows the horn, Ulandriel will appear before them in a vision and
ask their aid in completing his final, unfinished quest.  Every time the horn
is blown, all listen to hear the returning cry of a lonely ral'airdn that is
said will herald that one has been chosen.  And that, my friend, is the tale
of how Ulandriel came by the ral'airdn's horn.@n\"
"
!
