"spear1"
"With a ghostly wail $N recounts the tragic end of his life.  \"Faced with the
greate orcish horde bent on the destruction of all Khedrun I called my
greatest warriors to stand strong against the assault.  My people made me
proud that day and to a one they offered to stand.  The horde was too large
and had grown too strong and after a long and bloody day the orcish tribes
finally broke the spirit of my people.\"
"
E
"spear2"
"$N continues in a voice as raw as the sound of nails on slate \"My people,
bloody and dying, broke and fled across the Elhanni.  Though I knew it might
be the end I knew also that the people must be rallied and a final defense put
up or it would be the end.  The orcish horde would hunt us down to a person
and slaughter us all.  I raised my battle horn to my lips and rallied my men
for one last stand.  With so many dead or dying we made out stand at the
Bridge of Tears.\"
"
E
"spear3"
"The sepulchral voice of $N seems to hold a note of pride as he continues \"The
orcs did not expect that.  Even as we died we had already laid a hard price on
them.  Our final stand redoubled the cost.  With our life's breath and blood
we bought time.  As the sun was setting and I crossed the latest line of
fallen.  As I crossed the latest line of fallen, once again pushing the orcs
back across the Bridge of Tears so that the wounded could be retrieved and
tended to an orcish spear pierced my breast.\"
"
E
"spear4"
"With a voice like a dying wind $N continues \"Even as the sun fell so did I
with an orcish spear cruelly thrust through heart and lung.  With a final
breath I called on Duroth to aid me and Duroth gave me the choice.  Die a
hero's death and rest in honored peace or forego the peace of death for many
an age to buy my people the merest chance of survival.  It was not a choice
for me and as I stand here you see my choice.\"
"
E
"spear5"
"In a final dying rasp $N concludes \"So it is that I have stayed here, so near
the bridge where I once died, ever watchful for the return of the Durgoroth,
the orcish hordes of old.  Yet while I stand my ever vigilant watch my wounds
still pain me greatly.  I know not where the spear of my demise; the
Durgonell, lays but while it is yet in the world I can feel its life prying at
my essence always.  Perhaps one of my descendents, one of the Khedrun, will
know of it.  Seek among them for knowledge of the Durgonell.  Bring it to me
and I will give you my blessing.\"
"
